### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence clearly shows that the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) were conceived in July 1944 at the Bretton Woods Conference and officially established in December 1945. The historically recognized founders and key architects of these institutions were US Treasury Secretary Henry Morganthau, his chief economic advisor Harry Dexter White, and British economist John Maynard Keynes. Dr. Jose Rizal was executed in 1896, nearly five decades before these institutions were created, making his participation impossible. Furthermore, official IMF historical archives contain no mention of either Rizal or Ferdinand Marcos as founders or contributors, with Marcos only being mentioned in relation to subsequent economic deterioration and corruption during his dictatorship. Because this evidence is clear and definitive, no further investigation is required.

### Step 3: Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that President Ferdinand Marcos and Dr. Jose Rizal founded the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund is entirely false. Historical records show that the institutions were established decades after Rizal's death by American and British experts, and official archives do not credit either Marcos or Rizal with any role in their creation. Therefore, the final decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is completely false as the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) were conceived in July 1944 and [officially established in December 1945](https://pubdocs.worldbank.org/en/634901541019944926/70-years-prologue.pdf) by key architects [Henry Morganthau, Harry Dexter White, and John Maynard Keynes](https://www.brettonwoodsproject.org/2019/01/art-320747/). Dr. Jose Rizal could not have participated in their founding, as he was [executed in 1896](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rizal_Day), nearly five decades prior to the Bretton Woods Conference. Furthermore, [official IMF historical archives](https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/history/2001/ch01.pdf) contain no mention of Rizal or Ferdinand Marcos as founders, only referencing Marcos in connection to economic deterioration and corruption during his later dictatorship. While the [Commonwealth of the Philippines](https://publishing.cdlib.org/ucpressebooks/view?docId=ft658007bk;chunk.id=0;doc.view=print) was indeed one of the 44 nations represented at the conference, neither Marcos nor Rizal had any involvement in establishing these institutions.